Edition No. 29 — Thursday, 29 October 2026

10 stories: Twenty-four ministers in an afternoon: Hichilema fills the 30-ministry Cabinet, gives Tambatamba the mines and splits Energy between Kapala and Chikote (lead); A week's grace for the new ministers: the 2027 budget moves from Friday to 6 November, and the Vice-President says the government is 'still working on it'; K4.1 billion paid, K14.6 billion still owed: Musokotwane says most maize farmers will be paid in November once the Treasury releases K14.7 billion, and the FRA has export contracts; US$1.8 billion of suspected illicit flows, 580 tax cases and a shell company that turned K85 million into a K150 million contract: what the FIC's 2025 trends report says; 'We are around US$20 billion plus': Hichilema asks a Swiss trade mission to invest beyond copper and in joint ventures with Zambians, and thanks Bern for its part in the debt deal; A petition to scrap the two-term limit reaches Parliament through a UPND MP, Kabimba tells the President to silence those behind it, and the Attorney-General says time will tell; Third time, still not started: the High Court orders Mukobeko to produce Mundubile, Zulu and 16 others at 10:00 on Thursday after the defence asks for time to answer the State; Two down and then three up: Zambia's under-15s come back to beat England 3–2 in Baku and reach the last 16 of the first FIFA U-15 World Cup; Heroes is open for November: CAF grants the National Heroes Stadium temporary Category 3 approval, bringing the Burundi qualifier to Lusaka on 13 November and the U-23s' tie after; A ministry of its own: Christopher Chibuye of Mkushi North becomes the first Minister of Culture and Arts, five years after the arts were folded into Youth and Sport.

